  "answer_text": "## Explanation\n\nThe verifier enforces a hard cap of **512 bytes (`MAX_BPF_STACK`)** on the *combined* stack of any single call chain through global (non-inlined) functions.\n\nIn this program the deepest chain is:\n\n```\nentry  ->  helper_c  ->  helper_b  ->  helper_a\n```\n\n- `entry` uses ~24 bytes of stack\n- `helper_c` declares `volatile char buf[260]` \u2192 260 bytes\n- `helper_b` declares `volatile char buf[260]` \u2192 260 bytes\n- `helper_a` uses no stack\n\nSo the chain\u2019s combined stack is `24 + 260 + 260 = 544`, which exceeds 512. That is exactly what the log reports:\n\n```\ncombined stack size of 3 calls is 544. Too large\n```\n\n(The same applies to `entry -> helper_c -> helper_d`, also two 260-byte frames.)\n\nThis is not a logic bug \u2014 the functions verify fine individually (`helper_d` is reported safe). The only problem is the total stack budget across nested calls.\n\n## Fix\n\nShrink the per-function stack array so that two nested frames plus the entry frame stay under 512 bytes. We need:\n\n```\n2 * MAX_STACK + (entry stack \u2248 24) \u2264 512   =>   MAX_STACK \u2264 244\n```\n\nThe smallest source change is to reduce `MAX_STACK`:\n\n```c\n-#define MAX_STACK 260\n+#define MAX_STACK 240\n```\n\nWith `MAX_STACK = 240` the deepest chain uses `24 + 240 + 240 = 504 \u2264 512`, so verification passes while keeping the structure (multiple nested global functions each using a sizeable stack buffer) intact.\n\nIf the test\u2019s intent is specifically to probe the *boundary*, pick the largest value that still fits (e.g. `244`), but `240` gives a safe margin.",
